# R23 moors: the edge, the heath, the quagmire and the hardpan. Logic by hand, spoken text polished by Gemini (R23 moor), unvoiced.

~ sky
cutscene wv_grinning_storm
Narrator: The moor runs away from you in rain, and townsfolk run with it, some toward the village and some away. On a far hill a bonfire stands up against the clouds.
cutscene wv_grinning_storm_2
Narrator: The clouds coil into a grinning face with hollow eyes and lightning in its mouth. It looks straight down at you and tips its head, like a showman at his first guests.
crooked_man [sly]: Ohh, my heroes! Look at all that lovely road you have, and all those people on it. Save them all, will you? Do try. I have never seen it done.
cutscene end
set wv_sky_seen
quest wickers_vigil crooked_chaos
-> END

~ eyewitness
Narrator: Mori Shade stands in the rain over a body with a shovel, soaked through and perfectly still. Their white hair is plastered flat.
mori_shade [weary]: I came for the dead. There are more of them than I have graves. The living went north, all in a line, and none of them walked there on their own feet.
set wv_eyewitness_done
-> eyewitness_menu

~ eyewitness_menu
* Ask what they saw -> saw
* Ask who is still in the village -> village
* Ask about the fire on the hill -> fire
* Ask for a word on the road ahead -> road
* We will go on -> END

~ saw
mori_shade [neutral]: Masked villagers, hare and crow and stag, knocking people out and dragging them off. Monsters helping them. The mayor was gone at once. The constable was taken first.
mori_shade [sad]: Strangers came in from three towns, each with captives of their own. Everyone has gone toward the fire.
-> eyewitness_menu

~ village
mori_shade [neutral]: Tamsin has a queue and a barricade. Marianna is feeding folk in the inn yard. The sick are being moved to the Silent Church. It is not much, but it is something, and it is yours to look after.
-> eyewitness_menu

~ fire
mori_shade [afraid]: It is a man of willow sixty feet tall, with cages for a chest. They are not building a bonfire. They are building a person to burn.
-> eyewitness_menu

~ road
mori_shade [neutral]: Boneflayers over the heath. Something in the bog. Something worse on the hard ground after that. I did not go further than I could see.
-> eyewitness_menu

~ tracks
Narrator: Drag marks cut the peat in long parallel grooves, as if heels had been hauled through it. They all curve the same way, toward the hill.
* [Survival DC 13] Read the tracks -> tracks_ok | tracks_none
* Follow them by eye and count the drags -> tracks_count

~ tracks_ok
Narrator: Dozens of people, some walking and some carried, and among them the flat three-toed prints of something large that does not touch the ground much.
set wv_tracks_read
-> END

~ tracks_none
Narrator: The rain has been over the prints too long to read more than the direction.
-> END

~ tracks_count
Narrator: You count at least forty sets of heels before the rain washes the rest out. They all go north.
set wv_tracks_read
-> END

~ mask
Narrator: An antlered-hare mask of woven willow lies face up in the mud, the eyeholes ringed with soot.
* [Religion DC 14] Read the mask's marks -> mask_ok | mask_none
* Turn it over and study the stitching -> mask_turn

~ mask_ok
Narrator: The chants cut inside the rim name the Old Ways' Chosen: whoever wears the mask sings through it, and whoever tears it off silences the song.
set wv_mask_known
-> END

~ mask_none
Narrator: The marks are old and clumsy, and you cannot tell if the clumsiness is the carver's or the rain's.
-> END

~ mask_turn
Narrator: A mask grafted to a face, not tied on. Whoever wears these cannot take them off, but a strong hand might tear one away.
set wv_mask_known
-> END

~ leave_edge
Narrator: Ahead the moor drops into a long heath, and the smoke from the hill lies on it like a lid.
quest wickers_vigil death_from_above
set wv_edge_left
-> END

# ---- the heath ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

~ heath_start
Narrator: Open heather and scattered boulders. The smoke is coming down the hill in sheets, and in it, wide as a barn door, something large is turning. A line of villagers is running across the heath from the west.
set wv_h1_started
quest wickers_vigil death_from_above
* Look around for anything to use -> END
* Draw weapons and meet them -> heath_ready

~ heath_ready
Narrator: Six great shapes stoop out of the smoke on bone-white wings, beaks open, talons hanging. The villagers see them and scatter.
* Hold the heath against the boneflayers -> heath_fight
* Look about for something to use first -> END

~ heath_fight
Narrator: The first one takes a farmer by the shoulders and rises.
combat wv_boneflayers

~ gong
Narrator: A ploughman's gong hangs from a post by a chain, scoured by years of weather. It would ring like a church bell.
* Take the striker and bring the gong to the fight -> gong_take
* [Athletics DC 12] Haul the whole post to the middle of the heath -> gong_haul | gong_take
* Leave it -> END

~ gong_take
Narrator: You carry the striker and drag the gong's cord. A good strike will carry across the whole heath.
set wv_gong_ready
give wv_gong_striker
-> END

~ gong_haul
Narrator: The post comes out of the peat with the gong still on it, and you plant it where the fight will be. The first strike makes the heather shiver.
set wv_gong_ready
-> END

~ net
Narrator: A drover's net lies in the heather with its poles, the kind you hold between you to catch a falling sheep. Or a falling person.
* [Athletics DC 13] Rig the net on its poles for the villagers -> net_ok | net_poor
* [Survival DC 12] Set the net where they run -> net_ok | net_poor
* Take the poles and hold the net by hand -> net_hand

~ net_ok
Narrator: The net hangs taut between two poles, wide enough to catch anyone dropped over this stretch.
set wv_net_ready
-> END

~ net_poor
Narrator: The poles slip in the peat, and the net sags. It will catch someone, but it will not catch everyone.
set wv_net_ready
-> END

~ net_hand
Narrator: You and a fighter's strong arms, holding it between you. It will do for one or two.
set wv_net_ready
-> END

~ heath_walk
Narrator: Past the boulders the heath stays open for another stretch, and the shapes in the smoke are circling the running villagers. You could go on without turning.
* Hold the heath after all -> END
* Go on and leave the villagers to it -> heath_skip

~ heath_skip
Narrator: Behind you the shrieking begins. You do not look back.
set vigil_deaths += 5
set wv_h1_skipped
quest wickers_vigil death_from_below
-> END

~ heath_after
Narrator: The last boneflayer comes down in a tangle of wings. The heather is quiet except for rain.
if not flag.wv_h1_saved_1
set wv_h1_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h1_saved_2
set wv_h1_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h1_saved_3
set wv_h1_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h1_saved_4
set wv_h1_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h1_saved_5
set wv_h1_unsaved += 1
endif
-> heath_count

~ heath_count
if flag.wv_h1_unsaved == 0
if flag.wv_net_ready
set wv_h1_lost = 0
else
set wv_h1_lost = 0
endif
elif flag.wv_h1_unsaved == 1
if flag.wv_net_ready
set wv_h1_lost = 0
else
set wv_h1_lost = 1
set vigil_deaths += 1
endif
elif flag.wv_h1_unsaved == 2
if flag.wv_net_ready
set wv_h1_lost = 0
else
set wv_h1_lost = 2
set vigil_deaths += 2
endif
elif flag.wv_h1_unsaved == 3
if flag.wv_net_ready
set wv_h1_lost = 1
set vigil_deaths += 1
else
set wv_h1_lost = 3
set vigil_deaths += 3
endif
elif flag.wv_h1_unsaved == 4
if flag.wv_net_ready
set wv_h1_lost = 2
set vigil_deaths += 2
else
set wv_h1_lost = 4
set vigil_deaths += 4
endif
else
if flag.wv_net_ready
set wv_h1_lost = 3
set vigil_deaths += 3
else
set wv_h1_lost = 5
set vigil_deaths += 5
endif
endif
set wv_h1_tallied
-> heath_report

~ heath_report
if flag.wv_h1_lost == 0
Narrator: Every villager who ran is alive, shaking, and staring at you.
elif flag.wv_h1_lost <= 2
Narrator: Most of them live. A few blue-green motes lift from the heather and stream away toward the hill, and one by one the survivors look up to watch them go.
else
Narrator: Too many did not make it. Blue-green motes rise from the heather in a thread and stream north, and the hill takes them.
endif
-> END

~ potions
wv_fleeing_villager [smile]: You kept them off us. Take these, please. They were my father's, for the harvest.
give potion_of_healing_greater 2
set wv_potions_given
-> END

# ---- the quagmire -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

~ quag_start
Narrator: The rain has stopped. The bog lies black and still, and in it six villagers stand sunk to the waist, not speaking. The surface of the mud is moving in a line toward one of them.
set wv_h2_started
quest wickers_vigil death_from_below
* Look around for anything to use -> END
* Wade in -> quag_ready

~ quag_ready
Narrator: A wellwyrm's back breaks the mud and sinks again. There are more of them than the villagers.
* Fight the wellwyrms and pull the villagers out -> quag_fight
* Look about for something to use first -> END

~ quag_fight
Narrator: The line in the mud reaches the nearest villager.
combat wv_wellwyrms

~ horn
Narrator: A long drover's horn hangs from a post, the kind they blow to bring cattle across a bog. It would carry a mile in this stillness.
* Take the horn -> horn_take
* [Performance DC 12] Test a note on it -> horn_blow | horn_take
* Leave it -> END

~ horn_take
Narrator: You sling the horn on its cord. When the wyrms come, it will be there.
set wv_horn_ready
give wv_horn
-> END

~ horn_blow
Narrator: The note rolls out across the black water. Under it the mud stops moving, and does not move again for a long breath.
set wv_horn_ready
give wv_horn
-> END

~ poles
Narrator: Long poles, a coil of rope, a hurdle gate. Everything a hand needs to pull a person from a bog.
* Take the rope and poles -> poles_take
* Leave them -> END

~ poles_take
Narrator: The rope is in your hand. It will not make the bog shallower, but it will make pulling easier.
set wv_rope_ready
-> END

~ quag_walk
Narrator: Beyond the mud, the ground firms. The villagers are still sunk in it, six of them, and the mud is not going to stop.
* Stay and fight for them -> END
* Wade past and leave them -> quag_skip

~ quag_skip
Narrator: You pick a route over the hummocks. Behind you, one at a time, the mud takes them.
set vigil_deaths += 6
set wv_h2_skipped
quest wickers_vigil death_from_within
-> END

~ quag_after
Narrator: The last wellwyrm slides under and does not come up. The mud lies flat.
if not flag.wv_h2_saved_1
set wv_h2_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h2_saved_2
set wv_h2_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h2_saved_3
set wv_h2_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h2_saved_4
set wv_h2_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h2_saved_5
set wv_h2_unsaved += 1
endif
if not flag.wv_h2_saved_6
set wv_h2_unsaved += 1
endif
-> quag_count

~ quag_count
if flag.wv_h2_unsaved == 0
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 0
else
set wv_h2_lost = 0
endif
elif flag.wv_h2_unsaved == 1
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 0
else
set wv_h2_lost = 1
set vigil_deaths += 1
endif
elif flag.wv_h2_unsaved == 2
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 1
set vigil_deaths += 1
else
set wv_h2_lost = 2
set vigil_deaths += 2
endif
elif flag.wv_h2_unsaved == 3
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 2
set vigil_deaths += 2
else
set wv_h2_lost = 3
set vigil_deaths += 3
endif
elif flag.wv_h2_unsaved == 4
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 3
set vigil_deaths += 3
else
set wv_h2_lost = 4
set vigil_deaths += 4
endif
elif flag.wv_h2_unsaved == 5
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 4
set vigil_deaths += 4
else
set wv_h2_lost = 5
set vigil_deaths += 5
endif
else
if flag.wv_rope_ready
set wv_h2_lost = 5
set vigil_deaths += 5
else
set wv_h2_lost = 6
set vigil_deaths += 6
endif
endif
set wv_h2_tallied
-> quag_report

~ quag_report
if flag.wv_h2_lost == 0
Narrator: Six villagers sit on the bank, mud to the chest, alive. One of them starts to laugh and cannot stop.
elif flag.wv_h2_lost <= 2
Narrator: Most of them live. Blue-green motes slide up off the mud and go north, thin as breath.
else
Narrator: The bog gave back fewer than it took. Motes rise from the black water in a long line and stream away to the hill.
endif
-> END

~ lumen
Narrator: A priestess in a patched white cloak steps out of the reeds, singing under her breath. She looks at the survivors, counts them, and stops singing.
sister_lumen [smile]: Rest a moment. The Brightsingers owe you more than a song, and here is what I can give.
heal 3d8 party
inspiration party
set wv_lumen_done
Narrator: Warmth runs through you from her hands, three small healings, and for each of you a brightness in the chest that is like a held breath before a hymn.
-> END

# ---- the hardpan --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

~ pan_start
Narrator: Firmer ground at last, packed hard as a threshing floor. Rusted sickles and pitchforks lie where their owners dropped them. Four figures sit in the middle of the field, so still you took them for scarecrows. They are Foxwillow farmhands, and they are putting out shoots.
set wv_h3_started
quest wickers_vigil death_from_within
* Look around for anything to use -> END
* Walk toward them -> pan_ready

~ pan_ready
Narrator: The nearest one lifts a head heavy with bark, and where its eyes were, two buds are opening.
* Face the woodwarped -> pan_fight
* Look about for something to use first -> END

~ pan_fight
Narrator: The first claw comes up out of the ground like a root.
combat wv_woodwarped

~ axes
Narrator: A woodpile of axes and billhooks leans against a fence post, dropped by the farmhands when whatever it was came over them.
* Take an axe -> axes_take
* Leave them -> END

~ axes_take
Narrator: The woodwarped will see steel and think of the saw. A woodsman's axe cuts them like smoke.
give handaxe 2
set wv_axes_taken
-> END

~ pan_walk
Narrator: The path to the hill runs north across the hardpan, past the four sitting figures. Going around them is possible. Leaving them is not kind.
* Deal with the woodwarped -> END
* Go around and leave the four -> pan_skip

~ pan_skip
Narrator: One of the sitters turns its head to watch you pass. You keep walking.
set vigil_deaths += 4
set wv_h3_skipped
quest wickers_vigil ring_of_fire
-> END

~ pan_after
Narrator: The last woodwarped goes down in a shower of dry bark. Between the splinters, the farmhands who were not ruined lie breathing in the dirt, naked as the day they were planted.
set wv_h3_tallied
-> END

~ foot
Narrator: The path climbs. Ahead, the hymn comes down the hill through the dark like warm water through cold, and it has no words you can hold on to. The bonfire is a red wound on the cloud base.
set wv_foot_seen
quest wickers_vigil ring_of_fire
-> END

~ fall_back_foot
Narrator: The way back down is open. The village is a long road behind you, and the fire above is not going to wait for it.
-> vigil/square:retreat_confirm
